yload` as bounded encrypted/compressed payload rows until the runtime consumes and explicitly acknowledges those row ids.
+`device_sync_dirty_connection` is the coalescing point for high-cardinality device webhook backfills. It is keyed by hosted connection ID and tracks `dirty_revision`, `processed_revision`, first/latest dirty timestamps, widened safe windows, compact resource/source counters, and a compact `dirty_resources_json` map. It must not store raw provider request bodies, provider tokens, raw samples, or user-visible health facts. Provider-owned durable webhook work, such as Junction direct data or exact resource/delete/deauthorization jobs needed for later import, is event-triggered work and is stored in `device_sync_dirty_payload` as bounded encrypted/compressed payload rows until the runtime consumes and explicitly acknowledges those row ids. Every new encrypted row also stores the server-derived `credential_independent` authority bit. Classification, compression, and encryption finish before the admission transaction; the dirty-payload store performs only revision validation and database mutation after that transaction opens, while the outer owner retains its existing trace, mailbox, and signal commit. On a replacement `connectedAt` epoch, reconnect cleanup reads that bit set-wise: credential-scoped rows are deleted and credential-independent import carriers remain. Nullable rows are limited to mixed-version deployment and are classified outside the reconnect transaction before it may commit; reconnect never decrypts or dynamically loads provider code while holding the dirty-marker lock.
 
 The companion overnight PRV lane reuses that encrypted payload owner. Its only
